      {
        "id": 71443,
        "name": "Foote",
        "username": "Foote",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/foote/{size}/1877_2.png",
        "created_at": "2013-08-26T22:06:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Katsue\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/katsue/48/2012_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Katsue:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I personally don't consider a card to have been played before the text resolves, because the rulings do conflict, I think it's better of Hairtrigger Reflexes doesn't immediately shut down The Matriarch, and it makes more sense to me that powers and card plays should work the same way than that they shouldn't.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>If the Trigger was \"After a card is played\" instead of \"when a card is played\" I would agree with you.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>But it makes perfect sense that, at least in terms of Triggers, that \"using a power\" nessesarily entails the resolution of the power.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Hairtrigger has a Trigger that says \"when blah blah target enters play\". This to me reads different than Pos/Neg field that say \"when a card is played\". Is there, or should there be a difference here in timing? To me right now, I'd argue pos/neg fields only trigger after the effects of the card played are resolved, just like using a power. Hairtrigger seems dependent on just a target entering play (how does that shut matriarch down? Auto shredding fowls isn't exactly the best strat without meta gaming)</p>\n<p>Vigilance is worded the same as the fields are. So I guess I am calling into question whether Vigilance for Iron Legacy indeed deals damage before a card like Grease Gun would resolve.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Edit: Ronway, saw you said that a card enters play and is played basically are interchangeable phrases as far as mechanics go. Was this confirmed somewhere? Or just your general game sense?</p>",
