      {
        "id": 71450,
        "name": "arenson9",
        "username": "arenson9",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/arenson9/{size}/1900_2.png",
        "created_at": "2013-08-26T22:30:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>My initial intuition is that it seems strange that when a card is played, previously played cards can be triggered before the just-played card's effects are triggered, but that when a power is used, the power is resolved before previously played cards that trigger on a power use are taken into effect.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Considering, however, that using a power (except for base powers) requires two actions -- first play the card, then use the power -- I can explain away the difference using the perspective that since you have to expend twice as many resources (kind of), it's reasonable to win ties rather than lose them.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>On a related note, I think the point (brought up by Ronway just now?) that static reductions/increases/other changes on a just-played card might actually be in effect before a previously-played card triggers makes sense.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Sigh. I think I see another pending rules question coming ...</p>",
